    <!-- HELP.md (Fase 17): the second file behind the Settings tab bar. Prose, so
         FULLY localized, headings included. One item per rendered line: the document
         is markdown SOURCE, and Android collapses a real newline into a space. -->
    <string-array name="help_md">
        <item># tsteps</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>A step counter that presents itself as a code editor and a git repository: today is a file you read, and the past is its history. If the words below mean nothing to you, this page is for you.</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>## The four tabs</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>- **Editor** - `steps_data.json` and `README.md`: today. The first is the technical version, every field of it; the second is the same day told in words.</item>
        <item>- **Log** - `steps_history.diff` and `week.diff`: one entry per day, with each walk shown as the stretch of time it covered.</item>
        <item>- **Stats** - `stats.md`: totals, averages, and the coloured grid of the last weeks - one square per day, darker where you walked more.</item>
        <item>- **Settings** - `settings.config`: options change by tapping the value itself. There is no switch to hunt for.</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>## The borrowed words</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>- **commit** - a day, closed and filed at midnight. Once committed, a day no longer changes.</item>
        <item>- **working tree** - today: the one day still moving.</item>
        <item>- **diff** - what a day contains, written as changes. `+` lines are steps added, and a walk appears under the hours it covered.</item>
        <item>- **check** (`✓` / `✗`) - the daily goal, passed or missed: the test suite of a repository, run once a day.</item>
        <item>- **branch** (`⎇`) - the day you are looking at.</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>## Where the numbers come from</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>The phone\'s own step counter, the same sensor every other pedometer reads. tsteps has no network permission at all: nothing can leave the phone, because there is nowhere for it to go. Distance and calories are estimates worked out from your steps, and the file says so wherever they appear - the ones it cannot work out, it leaves out.</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>## Why it looks like this</item>
        <item></item>
        <item>Because a file can be read all at once, and because we like it this way. If you got this far, you already know whether it is for you.</item>
    </string-array>

</resources>
